  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Raising Economic and Social Security in agriculture for Child Labour Eradication</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Addressing child labor through saline farming and child friendly Upazila in Bangladesh</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Aquaculture in rural coastal regions of Bangladesh, combined with adverse climate change impacts, and high saltwater intrusion has been lowering the income and livelihood opportunities of poor and vulnerable communities in the country. The shrimp sector alone contributes roughly 4% to Bangladesh&apos;s GDP, yet the informality in shrimp aquaculture is associated with ecological problems and conflict as well as social and economic polarization that further exacerbates coastal inequality and poverty. At a local level, the sector has done little to address the human rights violations such as child and forced labor in key activities of the shrimp supply chain where nearly 1.2 million people directly and about 4.8 million indirectly depend on the sector for livelihood.
In activities such as shrimp fry catching, children are forced to engage to support family income at the expense of their education.  This is mainly because of lack of governmental interest in regulating this sector and dearth of infrastructure for sustainable farming of shrimps that relies on marginalized communities to fulfil the trade demands. In the year 2000, the government enacted a ban on wild-caught fry collection, in recognition of environmental concerns and to promote hatchery production but thousands of children living in the coastal districts are still involved in shrimp farming to support themselves and their families. 
The issue of child labor in shrimp sector is an outcome of a myriad of factors that need to be addressed in order to find not only a sustainable but an integrated approach. New and improved income options are desperately needed for the coastal poor men and women of Bangladesh if child labor is to be addressed. Additionally, the income strategies to prevent child labor and ensure their withdrawal and continued learning relies on climate resilient ecosystems and livelihood practices targeting high risk areas.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project intends to engage at least 50% women beneficiaries and 2% people with disability.
Direct Beneficiaries:
1. 360 aquaculture&#x2002;Household (210 shrimp farmers including 75 fry collectors)
2. 540 school-going aged children
3. Total 440&#x2002;Parents, school management committee members, union disaster management committee members, market actors

Indirect Beneficiaries: 1800 households</narrative>
  </description>
